summary:
MARS-SOIL is an extraterrestrial geotechnical engineering project that seeks to answer one question: how reliable are Martian terrains for construction? Not all regions offer the same conditions to support a habitable module, a landing pad or a radiation-shielding structure.

The underlying problem is the lack of data. Direct measurements exist for only a few points visited by missions (Viking, Spirit, Opportunity, Curiosity, Perseverance, InSight), and for most of the planet the properties of the soil are known only indirectly or not at all.

For that reason, the project does not seek a single value, but rather an evaluation methodology that makes it possible to:

compare regions using a common criterion;
draw on terrestrial analog sites where measurements do exist;
distinguish between measured, estimated and unknown data;
guide decisions before foundation design.

The deliverable is that methodology, with a suitability index and a procedure to assess the reliability of the information used.
